    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument that gaps are biologically rooted often relies on the concept of \"percentile rank\" differences remaining stable across various environmental settings. Proponents suggest that if the difference were purely environmental, societal changes would rapidly equalize test scores. They posit that genetic factors contribute to differential cognitive potential, meaning some groups possess a heritable predisposition for higher or lower cognitive function. This viewpoint frames observed disparities as markers of inherent, genetically influenced variance.",
